{-
   Extended grapheme clusters - Implementation-String.md part 8.2, and UAX #29 at the version
   `unicodeVersion` names.

   A cluster is a `String` window and has no type of its own: `clusters` yields them, and the
   boundary functions answer offsets that compose with `String.slice`. `Clusters.ownership.yana`
   and `Clusters.legacy.yana` hold the two functions that hand out a piece of the text, for the same
   reason `Core`'s trims are split: a window is `source'` on the ownership stage and a copy on the
   legacy one.

   **Invalid text has clusters too.** A character is what `decodeChar` answers, so each invalid
   sequence is a U+FFFD, which is `Other` and is a cluster of its own unless a mark follows it.

   ## The break classes

   Each character has one of eighteen classes, looked up by `clusterClassAt` (`Tables.yana`). They
   are the `Grapheme_Cluster_Break` values with two of them split, so that every rule below is a test
   on classes rather than on a second property: `Extend` by its `Indic_Conjunct_Break` value, which
   GB9c needs, and `Other` into its `Extended_Pictographic` and `InCB=Consonant` characters, which
   GB11 and GB9c need. The generator checks that both splits are partitions of the UCD's data.

        0 Other            4 Extend           9 Prepend               13 T
        1 CR               5 ConjunctExtend  10 Regional_Indicator    14 LV
        2 LF               6 ConjunctLinker  11 L                     15 LVT
        3 Control          7 ZWJ             12 V                     16 Pictographic
                           8 SpacingMark                              17 Consonant

   `tools/unicode/generate.py`'s `CLUSTER_CLASSES` is the same list, and the two move together.
   ZWJ is `InCB=Extend` in the data and keeps its own class, which GB9c reads as a conjunct extender.

   ## The rules as two functions

   UAX #29's rules are ordered, and every rule from GB3 to GB9b looks at the two characters beside a
   position and nothing else. That is `pairBreaks`. The three that look further back - GB9c, GB11 and
   GB12/GB13 - only ever *join*, and only where `pairBreaks` would break; they are `joinedByContext`,
   over three pieces of state that the forward walk carries. None of the three can be overridden by
   GB4 or GB5, because the characters they join are never controls, so "the pair breaks and no
   context joins" is the ordered list's answer.

   **A position where the pair breaks and no context rule could join is a boundary whatever came
   before it.** That is what makes the boundary functions cheap: they walk backwards to such a
   position with no state, and then forwards from it. The walk back is as long as the run of
   characters that can join across a position - a cluster's marks, or a run of regional indicators,
   which is the only unbounded case in real text.
-}

let crClass = 1 :: U8
let regionalClass = 10 :: U8
let pictographicClass = 16 :: U8
let consonantClass = 17 :: U8

-- An ASCII unit's class, with no lookup: ASCII has no marks, pictographs or conjuncts.
fn asciiClass(unit: CodeUnit) -> U8
  | "\r"          -> 1
  | "\n"          -> 2
  | "\0"..="\x1F" -> 3
  | "\x7F"        -> 3
  | _             -> 0

-- The class of the character at `at`, and the offset after it.
@inline(always)
fn classAt(text: String, at: Size) -> {U8, Size}:
    let unit = text.unit(at)
    if unit < 0x80 then return {asciiClass(unit), at + 1}

    let {char, next} = decodeChar(text, at)
    return {clusterClassAt(char), next}

-- GB3 to GB9b: whether the pair alone breaks.
fn pairBreaks(before: U8, after: U8) -> Bool
  | {1, 2}             -> False  -- GB3   CR × LF
  | {1..=3, _}         -> True   -- GB4   (Control | CR | LF) ÷
  | {_, 1..=3}         -> True   -- GB5   ÷ (Control | CR | LF)
  | {11, 11..=12}      -> False  -- GB6   L × (L | V | LV | LVT)
  | {11, 14..=15}      -> False
  | {12, 12..=13}      -> False  -- GB7   (LV | V) × (V | T)
  | {14, 12..=13}      -> False
  | {13, 13}           -> False  -- GB8   (LVT | T) × T
  | {15, 13}           -> False
  | {_, 4..=8}         -> False  -- GB9   × (Extend | ZWJ), and GB9a × SpacingMark
  | {9, _}             -> False  -- GB9b  Prepend ×
  | _                  -> True

{-
   GB9c, GB11 and GB12/GB13: whether what came before joins `after` to it.

   - `conjunct` is GB9c's: 1 after a consonant followed only by conjunct extenders and linkers, 2 once
     one of those was a linker. A consonant after a 2 joins.
   - `emoji` is GB11's: 1 after a pictograph followed only by `Extend`, 2 when a ZWJ ended that run.
     A pictograph after a 2 joins.
   - `oddRegional` is GB12's and GB13's: whether the run of regional indicators ending just before
     is of odd length. A regional indicator after an odd run joins, which pairs flags from the left.
-}
fn joinedByContext(after: U8, conjunct: U8, emoji: U8, oddRegional: Bool) -> Bool
  | {17, 2, _, _}    -> True
  | {16, _, 2, _}    -> True
  | {10, _, _, True} -> True
  | _                -> False

-- Whether a pair that breaks is a boundary without knowing anything before it: the pairs a context
-- rule could join are a conjunct extender or linker before a consonant, a ZWJ before a pictograph,
-- and two regional indicators.
fn contextCanJoin(before: U8, after: U8) -> Bool
  | {5..=7, 17} -> True
  | {7, 16}     -> True
  | {10, 10}    -> True
  | _           -> False

@inline(always)
fn conjunctAfter(state: U8, next: U8) -> U8
  | {_, 17}     -> 1
  | {0, 6}      -> 0
  | {_, 6}      -> 2
  | {_, 5}      -> state
  | {_, 7}      -> state
  | _           -> 0

@inline(always)
fn emojiAfter(state: U8, next: U8) -> U8
  | {_, 16}     -> 1
  | {1, 4..=6}  -> 1
  | {1, 7}      -> 2
  | _           -> 0

{-
   The end of the cluster that starts at `from`, which must be a boundary.

   The common case never reaches the loop: an ASCII unit other than CR followed by another ASCII
   unit (or by the end) is a cluster of one, because nothing that can join a character to the one
   before it is ASCII.
-}
fn clusterEnd(text: String, from: Size) -> Size:
    let count = length(text)
    if from >= count then return count

    let lead = text.unit(from)
    if lead < 0x80 && lead != "\r" && (from + 1 == count || text.unit(from + 1) < 0x80) then return from + 1

    let {first, second} = classAt(text, from)
    let &before = first
    let &at = second
    let &conjunct = conjunctAfter(0, before)
    let &emoji = emojiAfter(0, before)
    let &oddRegional = before == regionalClass

    while at < count:
        let {after, next} = classAt(text, at)
        if pairBreaks(before, after) && !joinedByContext(after, conjunct, emoji, oddRegional) then return at

        conjunct = conjunctAfter(conjunct, after)
        emoji = emojiAfter(emoji, after)
        oddRegional = after == regionalClass && !oddRegional
        before = after
        at = next

    return count

-- A boundary at or before `at`, which is a character boundary inside the text: the nearest position
-- whose pair breaks with no context rule able to join it, or the start.
fn restartBefore(text: String, at: Size) -> Size:
    let &position = at

    while position > 0:
        let {after, next} = classAt(text, position)
        let {char, start} = decodeCharBefore(text, position)
        let before = clusterClassAt(char)
        if pairBreaks(before, after) && !contextCanJoin(before, after) then return position

        position = start

    return 0

-- The start of the cluster holding the unit at `at`: `at` itself when it begins one. An offset
-- before the text is its start and one past it is its end, as with `floorCharBoundary`.
pub fn floorClusterBoundary(text: String, at: Size) -> Size:
    let count = length(text)
    if at <= 0 then return 0
    if at >= count then return count

    let &start = restartBefore(text, floorCharBoundary(text, at))
    let &end = clusterEnd(text, start)
    while end <= at:
        start = end
        end = clusterEnd(text, start)

    return start

-- The end of the cluster holding the unit at `at`, or `at` when a cluster begins there.
pub fn ceilClusterBoundary(text: String, at: Size) -> Size:
    let start = floorClusterBoundary(text, at)
    if start >= at then return start

    return clusterEnd(text, start)

pub fn isClusterBoundary(text: String, at: Size) -> Bool =
    at >= 0 && at <= length(text) && floorClusterBoundary(text, at) == at
